## Terminals and SDKs

This kit turns on terminal coverage. Command-line tools, SDKs and command-line
agents (curl, Python, Node, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, Codex) cannot read
the proxy (PAC) file and mostly keep their own certificate list, so the agent
sets them up itself:

- It writes a certificate bundle (the system's own roots plus the agent's
  certificate) and points `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`, `SSL_CERT_FILE` and
  `CURL_CA_BUNDLE` at it, and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` at the agent's certificate.
- It sets `HTTPS_PROXY` to the agent for new terminals. Sites other than AI
  services pass through the agent untouched and are never decrypted.
- Terminals never wait on the agent: on a Mac, a new terminal goes direct while
  the agent is down; on Windows, the settings are removed when the agent stops.
- A value your company already sets (a proxy or a certificate bundle) is kept.
  An employee can opt out in a terminal with `export VOTAL_TERMINAL_PROXY=off`
  (Mac); this is a default, not a control.

`votal-device-agent verify` reports "terminal coverage" on each laptop.
